## Maintenance evidence and interpretations

The actual-v1 test launches the received binary, commits one receipt, admits and
durably cancels a large pending job, and kills that writer. Independent RocksDB
inspection confirms pending state before starting v2. V2 recovers it, returns
existing for a retry with different priority, accepts new high work, preserves
the old receipt byte-for-byte, and drains. Both normal and race runs passed.

The general crash tests now alternate high/normal priority and inspect priority
metadata after each stopped writer. Pending count and byte sums, contiguous
checkpoint/receipt/pending records, id indexes and cancellations remain checked
independently of service status. The socket suite mixes priorities across eight
clients and retries with the opposite priority while requiring existing identity.

The success bitmap uses least-significant bit i for item i; the supplied header
does not explain bit order, but both full-width patterns agree with per-item
statuses. Arena backing remains native-owned until return. Missing legacy
priority means normal; invalid explicit priority fails. Evaluate uses optional
priority in the shared queues. Scheduling restarts high-first and does not add
disk metadata. These choices and remaining specification ambiguities are recorded
in HANDOFF.md; no clarification was requested.

Go race checks cover exercised Go code, not C/C++ libraries. Strict cgo checks
cover pointer rules, not a native memory-safety proof. No ASan, native-entry barrier
trace, forced allocation/storage failure, exact admission-limit saturation, or
power-loss simulation was added. Production queue tests establish the intended
dispatch choices but do not prove an externally observed native call-entry order
under arbitrary OS scheduling. No service test hooks or evaluator access was used.

